from reading the query alone.\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database has to read every row and lower-case each email before comparing. This is the worst problem, because the filter is meant to return a single user.\n2. **Possible full scan of `orders`.** The join runs on `o.user_id`. If there's no index on that column, the database either scans `orders` once per matching user (nested loop) or builds a hash of the whole table. Foreign keys aren't indexed automatically in Postgres or MySQL/InnoDB (InnoDB creates one implicitly, Postgres doesn't).\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables and repeats the user columns on every order row. It also blocks index-only scans, and any wide or TOAST columns (text, JSON) get read for no reason.\n4. **Unbounded result.** There's no `LIMIT` or `ORDER BY`. A user with many orders returns all of them.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Make the email lookup indexable.** Pick one:\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: expression index (keeps the query as written)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- Option B: case-insensitive type (Postgres), then query WHERE u.email = '...'\n-- ALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN email TYPE citext;\n-- CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_idx ON users (email);\n\n-- Option C: normalize emails to lower case on write, index plain email,\n-- and drop the lower() from the query.\n```\n\nI'd go with Option A as the lowest-risk change, since it needs no data or app changes. Make it `UNIQUE` only if you already treat emails as unique case-insensitively. Check for existing duplicates first.\n\n**2. Index the join key.**\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n-- If you usually want newest first:\n-- CREATE INDEX orders_user_id_created_idx ON orders (user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\n**3. Rewrite to select only what's needed, and bound it.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.status, o.total, o.created_at   -- the columns you actually use\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nWith both indexes, the plan should be an index lookup on `users` (one row) followed by an index range scan on `orders`. That replaces two full-table scans.\n\n## Verify\n\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. You should see `Seq Scan on users` change to `Index Scan using users_email_lower_idx`, and `orders` change to an index or bitmap scan on `user_id`. If your database isn't Postgres, tell me which one and I'll adjust the syntax. The expression-index syntax differs in MySQL (8.0.13+ functional indexes) and SQL Server (computed column).", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}